WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.560
Imagine being told to build this massive permanent bridge to an island.

00:05.480 --> 00:11.000
But right after you get the order, the island's owners announced they were going to blow up their side of the bridge.

00:11.160 --> 00:12.680
Right, which is already bad enough.

00:12.820 --> 00:19.900
But then, to make it worse, you still completely rely on them for daily shipments of supplies just to maintain your half of the bridge.

00:19.960 --> 00:23.820
Exactly. I mean, if you lose connection to that island, your project is dead.

00:24.380 --> 00:29.440
But if you try to force a connection the wrong way, the whole thing just collapses under its own weight.

00:29.440 --> 00:33.400
Yeah, it's an incredibly hostile architectural constraint.

00:34.100 --> 00:40.400
It really forces a complete rethinking of how we handle open source software, especially at an enterprise scale.

00:40.800 --> 00:43.720
So today we are looking at an engineering nightmare.

00:44.640 --> 00:47.400
Or, well, an engineering marvel, depending on how you look at it.

00:47.800 --> 00:51.740
If you're listening to this, I want you to put yourself in the shoes of a newly hired engineer.

00:51.880 --> 00:54.820
Oh, yeah. The ultimate onboarding mission.

00:55.220 --> 00:58.680
Right. You've just been handed the keys to this massive system.

00:58.680 --> 01:04.920
You are onboarding as a new service owner for the Azure SPI, the service provider interface.

01:05.440 --> 01:07.340
Your mission isn't just to write code.

01:07.840 --> 01:10.380
You need to understand exactly how this machine works.

01:10.660 --> 01:13.140
And why it was built the way it was.

01:13.220 --> 01:17.940
Because you have to keep it running when the upstream community decides to pull the rug out from under you.

01:17.940 --> 01:19.260
Which they will do.

01:19.800 --> 01:21.180
So welcome to the deep dive.

01:21.680 --> 01:25.160
To get you up to speed, we're extracting insights from the internal training manual.

01:25.600 --> 01:26.920
It's called the Complete Guide.

01:27.280 --> 01:30.060
From an upstream commit to a running Azure platform.

01:30.220 --> 01:32.860
We are going to analyze their architectural decision records.

01:33.080 --> 01:36.680
Look at some, frankly, incredibly brutal admissions of failure.

01:36.680 --> 01:40.700
And explore the ingenious solutions this team eventually landed on.

01:40.940 --> 01:43.020
And we aren't just looking at how code runs here.

01:43.080 --> 01:44.640
We're looking at a survival mechanism.

01:45.160 --> 01:49.180
But, you know, to really grasp why this architecture is so intensely complicated,

01:49.680 --> 01:52.180
we've got to start with the industry problem it's trying to solve.

01:52.340 --> 01:56.620
Right. Because you don't build a system this complex unless the alternative is just a total disaster.

01:57.020 --> 01:57.380
Exactly.

01:57.760 --> 02:00.040
We have to talk about the energy industry's data problem.

02:00.040 --> 02:08.960
Yeah. If you step into the energy sector, you immediately run into a data fragmentation issue that makes standard tech debt look, well, it makes it look trivial.

02:09.100 --> 02:09.720
It's massive.

02:09.920 --> 02:13.320
It is, at its core, this huge translation problem.

02:13.780 --> 02:16.500
Imagine a single major energy operator, right?

02:16.520 --> 02:20.300
They have seismic surveys, well, logs, geological interpretations.

02:20.820 --> 02:24.060
And, well, log isn't just a simple spreadsheet.

02:24.060 --> 02:27.720
We're talking about massive, multidimensional data sets, right?

02:28.120 --> 02:33.220
Showing acoustic impedance, gamma ray radiation, resistivity, all mapped against depth.

02:33.380 --> 02:33.860
Exactly.

02:34.180 --> 02:36.240
And this data has been gathered over decades.

02:36.460 --> 02:40.440
It's stored in dozens of completely incompatible proprietary systems.

02:40.860 --> 02:46.860
It's produced by competing vendors who all have radically different ideas about what a well actually is in a database schema.

02:47.020 --> 02:48.000
Wait, how different could it be?

02:48.000 --> 02:57.200
Oh, wildly different. Vendor A might define a well by its surface coordinates, while vendor B defines it by the specific wellbore trajectory underground.

02:57.600 --> 03:03.000
Wow. So if two energy companies merge, they have to merge these massive, chaotic archives.

03:03.120 --> 03:13.980
Or even if, like, a data scientist just wants to adopt a new machine learning tool to find untapped resources, they have to write yet another custom adapter for every single proprietary database.

03:14.140 --> 03:15.380
It is an absolute nightmare.

03:15.380 --> 03:20.120
You'd spend 90% of your time just reformatting data instead of analyzing it.

03:20.420 --> 03:23.880
And the industry's collective answer to this is OSDU.

03:24.120 --> 03:25.760
The open subsurface data universe.

03:25.980 --> 03:33.140
Right. It's an open standard, but more importantly for us, it's an open source reference implementation for a common data platform.

03:33.360 --> 03:36.000
It lives in GitLab. It's maintained by this vast community.

03:36.260 --> 03:39.560
And it contains all these core services to standardize the industry.

03:39.860 --> 03:41.460
So what kind of services are we talking about?

03:41.460 --> 03:50.260
You have a storage service for records, a schema service that defines what those records actually look like, a search service, legal compliance, and entitlements for authorization.

03:50.800 --> 03:53.720
So OSDU is basically the universal translator for the whole industry.

03:53.880 --> 03:53.980
Yep.

03:54.160 --> 03:56.360
But here's the massive structural constraint, right?

03:56.500 --> 03:56.640
Yeah.

03:56.780 --> 03:59.160
OSDU is designed to run on any cloud.

03:59.160 --> 04:09.940
Yes. And that creates a lot of friction because abstract logic is great, but eventually a storage service needs to actually save a file to a real hard drive.

04:10.080 --> 04:16.400
Right. An entitlement service needs to query a real graph database to see who belongs to what group.

04:16.540 --> 04:21.120
Precisely. You can't abstract away the physical reality of cloud infrastructure forever.

04:21.120 --> 04:27.080
So OSDU's answer to this multi-cloud ambition is something they call the provider model.

04:27.280 --> 04:28.620
Okay. Break that down for us.

04:28.620 --> 04:33.320
The business logic of each service, the core rules of how the energy data behaves, is shared.

04:33.740 --> 04:41.720
But the cloud-specific plumbing, the actual code that talks to a database or a storage bucket, is pushed behind swappable implementations.

04:41.940 --> 04:45.220
So if I'm looking at the source code in GitLab, what do I actually see?

04:45.480 --> 04:47.820
You'll see a shared module, for example, partition core.

04:47.820 --> 04:54.500
This contains the universal rules. And right next to it, in the exact same repository, you'll see provider directories.

04:55.000 --> 04:56.540
Like folders for each cloud.

04:56.580 --> 05:03.960
Exactly. You'll see provider partition Azure, provider partition AS, provider partition GC, one set of universal rules, but multiple sets of highly specific plumbing.

05:04.240 --> 05:10.700
You know, I always think of this like buying a universal remote control for a really complicated home theater system.

05:10.800 --> 05:11.880
That's a good way to look at it.

05:11.880 --> 05:18.980
Yeah. The remote itself, the buttons, the volume dial, the core logic, is exactly the same for everyone who buys it.

05:19.500 --> 05:28.900
But the infrared codes, like the specific signals it blasts out, those have to be meticulously programmed for your specific brand of television.

05:29.060 --> 05:32.780
Right. The provider directories are those specific infrared codes.

05:32.780 --> 05:36.620
Which brings us to this acronym that is plastered all over the documentation.

05:36.940 --> 05:37.340
SPI.

05:37.960 --> 05:39.640
Yes. The service provider interface.

05:39.880 --> 05:46.820
And we really need to untangle that acronym right now because confusing the terminology here makes the rest of the architecture impossible to follow.

05:46.980 --> 05:48.600
Because it means like three different things, right?

05:48.680 --> 05:53.180
It does. The abbreviation SPI is heavily overloaded in this ecosystem.

05:53.560 --> 05:57.500
And as a new service owner, you have to know which one you're talking about at any given time.

05:57.620 --> 05:59.240
Okay. Let's break them down for the listener.

05:59.240 --> 06:03.380
First, there's the interface itself. That's OSDU's pluggability seam.

06:03.860 --> 06:08.660
It is the literal boundary in the code between the shared logic and the cloud implementation.

06:09.160 --> 06:13.440
It's the contract. That interface belongs entirely to the open source community.

06:13.560 --> 06:15.180
Exactly. That's the shape of the plug.

06:15.640 --> 06:19.500
Second, there is the engineering system, which the team named OzDoSPI.

06:19.840 --> 06:24.740
This is the Azure-owned GitHub project that keeps the Azure provider code alive.

06:24.860 --> 06:26.800
It synchronizes it with the upstream community.

06:26.800 --> 06:30.260
Yes. And ultimately produces the deployable container images.

06:30.580 --> 06:32.360
So OSDU's PIs is essentially the factory.

06:32.540 --> 06:33.640
Exactly. It's the factory.

06:34.120 --> 06:37.040
And third, there is the stack, OSDU's PIs stack.

06:37.160 --> 06:38.280
That's the deployment platform.

06:38.480 --> 06:43.260
It's a completely separate code base that provisions the actual Azure infrastructure.

06:43.480 --> 06:45.760
The databases, the Kubernetes clusters, all that stuff.

06:45.820 --> 06:49.700
Right. And it runs the environment where those container images live.

06:49.820 --> 06:52.180
Okay. So we have the interface, which is the contract.

06:52.380 --> 06:52.560
Yeah.

06:52.720 --> 06:54.380
The engineering system, which is the factory.

06:54.380 --> 06:54.860
Yeah.

06:54.860 --> 06:56.600
And the stack, the infrastructure.

06:56.700 --> 06:57.220
We've got it.

06:57.360 --> 07:02.580
But, you know, if OSDU is an open source community project, my first instinct as an engineer is to say,

07:02.640 --> 07:07.680
well, why can't Azure just submit their remote codes, their provider implementations,

07:07.900 --> 07:11.200
directly to the main GitLab repository and be done with it?

07:11.300 --> 07:12.200
That would be the easy way.

07:12.520 --> 07:12.780
Yeah.

07:12.780 --> 07:17.440
Why do they need this massive separate engineering factory just to maintain a fork?

07:17.960 --> 07:22.540
Well, that brings us back to that existential threat that drives the entire design of this architecture.

07:23.260 --> 07:28.280
Upstream OSDU intends to delete its Azure provider implementations entirely.

07:28.540 --> 07:29.120
Wait, what?

07:29.320 --> 07:29.540
Yeah.

07:29.680 --> 07:33.360
They're removing them from the shared community repository permanently.

07:34.040 --> 07:35.100
Why would they do that?

07:35.100 --> 07:41.320
Because maintaining cloud-specific code inside a shared repository creates an enormous bottleneck.

07:41.720 --> 07:48.860
Every time AWS, Google, or Azure wants to update their specific database connector, it clutters the core repository.

07:49.120 --> 07:49.580
Oh, I see.

07:49.640 --> 07:52.040
So the community made a massive structural decision.

07:52.200 --> 07:55.400
They said, we are only going to host the core logic in the interfaces.

07:56.120 --> 07:59.820
You, the cloud providers, have to host your own implementations somewhere else.

08:00.020 --> 08:00.380
Wow.

08:00.380 --> 08:06.180
After that dilution happens, the directory provider partition Azure simply will not exist upstream at all.

08:06.340 --> 08:07.960
So they really are blowing up the bridge.

08:08.040 --> 08:08.640
That's wild.

08:08.800 --> 08:09.200
They are.

08:09.340 --> 08:13.280
And this forces Azure to invert the usual relationship you have with the code fork.

08:13.580 --> 08:17.760
Because usually, if you think about how we normally use a fork in software development,

08:18.540 --> 08:24.500
you fork a repository because you are worried about the upstream project changing code that you depend on.

08:24.560 --> 08:24.800
Right.

08:24.900 --> 08:29.620
Or because you want to build a feature in isolation without messing up the main branch.

08:29.620 --> 08:29.940
Yeah.

08:30.020 --> 08:33.280
You take a snapshot, you walk away, and maybe you occasionally pull in some bug fixes.

08:33.460 --> 08:39.900
But here, Azure must take permanent sole ownership of a specific subtree, the Azure provider code,

08:40.040 --> 08:45.820
while simultaneously continuing to receive daily mandatory updates for the shared core logic.

08:45.980 --> 08:47.820
And all the community integration tests, too.

08:47.940 --> 08:48.300
Exactly.

08:48.520 --> 08:52.740
If they stop taking upstream changes for the core logic, they drift out of compliance,

08:52.960 --> 08:55.040
and they effectively stop being OSDU.

08:55.460 --> 08:58.560
So a standard snapshot fork is completely out of the question.

08:58.560 --> 09:03.460
You are forced to maintain a permanent living relationship with the upstream code.

09:03.760 --> 09:04.060
Yes.

09:04.180 --> 09:08.660
But you can't just click sync every day because you own a piece of the code that they don't have anymore.

09:08.920 --> 09:09.420
Precisely.

09:09.960 --> 09:12.540
And if you try to merge those two realities by hand,

09:12.920 --> 09:18.340
the sheer cost of resolving code conflicts every single day across a dozen microservices will,

09:18.880 --> 09:21.440
it will eventually crush the engineering team.

09:21.440 --> 09:28.720
The documentation actually goes into great detail about how their first attempt at doing this failed spectacularly.

09:29.140 --> 09:32.620
And I think this is a perfect lesson for anyone dealing with Git at scale.

09:32.740 --> 09:33.740
Oh, it's a brutal lesson.

09:33.980 --> 09:34.140
Yeah.

09:34.160 --> 09:39.160
I was reading the architectural decision records, and ADR0001 outlines this failure.

09:39.600 --> 09:41.280
Let's talk about the modified LEET loop.

09:41.640 --> 09:45.520
It's a brilliant, albeit painful, case study in Git mechanics.

09:45.520 --> 09:51.160
When they first realized they had to maintain this permanent fork, they tried the obvious solution.

09:51.300 --> 09:51.780
Which was what?

09:51.860 --> 09:53.740
The initial naive strategy was this.

09:54.340 --> 09:59.540
Take the upstream code, manually delete the provider directories you don't need, like AWS or Google Cloud,

09:59.660 --> 10:01.640
and commit those deletions to your Azure fork.

10:01.740 --> 10:03.100
Okay, so now my fork is clean.

10:03.260 --> 10:05.280
It just has the core logic and the Azure code.

10:05.360 --> 10:05.560
Right.

10:05.560 --> 10:11.600
And then the plan was to set up an automated daily job to merge the new upstream commits into the Azure fork.

10:11.920 --> 10:15.520
They used a standard Git command, Git merge nax theirs.

10:15.920 --> 10:20.620
Which basically tells Git, hey, if there's a conflict between what I have and what upstream has,

10:20.940 --> 10:23.540
just assume upstream is right and overwrite my stuff.

10:23.760 --> 10:24.180
Exactly.

10:24.680 --> 10:29.680
The logic was, we want to blindly accept all upstream changes to the core logic.

10:29.880 --> 10:32.240
And it sounds perfectly reasonable on paper.

10:32.480 --> 10:33.640
But I'm guessing it doesn't work.

10:33.640 --> 10:38.020
It completely falls apart the moment you hit a tree-level structural conflict,

10:38.540 --> 10:40.840
specifically a modified elite conflict.

10:41.220 --> 10:45.160
Okay, walk me through a day in the life of a modified elite conflict.

10:45.380 --> 10:46.340
What actually happens?

10:46.560 --> 10:47.560
Let's say it's Tuesday.

10:48.080 --> 10:54.620
Upstream merges a pull request that modifies, I don't know, line 42 of a file inside the AWS provider directory.

10:54.860 --> 10:55.100
Okay.

10:55.340 --> 11:00.180
But remember, in your Azure fork, you explicitly deleted that entire AWS directory last week.

11:00.180 --> 11:06.600
When your automated nightly sync runs Git merge, Git's internal engine looks at the working tree and just halts.

11:06.920 --> 11:08.060
As it's confused.

11:08.660 --> 11:12.500
Git is basically saying upstream wants me to change line 42 of this file.

11:12.700 --> 11:15.420
But you, Azure, told me this file shouldn't even exist.

11:15.740 --> 11:16.040
Yes.

11:16.780 --> 11:21.000
Git doesn't know whether the correct architectural answer is to apply the modification,

11:21.000 --> 11:25.140
meaning it has to recreate the file and the directory you specifically deleted,

11:25.380 --> 11:29.360
or to respect your previous deletion and throw away the upstream modification.

11:29.680 --> 11:32.280
But wait, doesn't the ASX-THIRS flag fix that?

11:32.720 --> 11:33.840
No, that's the catch.

11:34.160 --> 11:39.700
The ASX-THIRS flag only auto-resolves content conflicts inside a file that exists in both places.

11:39.700 --> 11:44.920
It cannot resolve a structural conflict where a file exists in one reality and is deleted in the other.

11:45.200 --> 11:48.080
It halts the pipeline and asks a human to decide.

11:48.080 --> 11:51.920
And if you've ever tried to resolve a massive Git conflict on a Friday afternoon,

11:52.180 --> 11:54.060
you know exactly how painful this is.

11:54.420 --> 11:57.320
Now imagine your CI pipeline doing that to you every single day.

11:57.400 --> 11:58.040
It's exhausting.

11:58.660 --> 12:00.980
So a human engineer gets alerted, the pipeline is broken.

12:01.520 --> 12:02.400
What does the human do?

12:02.840 --> 12:07.840
Well, humans, being humans, usually just want the merge to finish so they can unblock the bill and go to lunch.

12:07.940 --> 12:08.100
Right.

12:08.260 --> 12:13.680
So they look at the conflict, they might not fully understand the context of the upstream AWS change,

12:13.840 --> 12:15.840
and they just run Git add A.

12:15.840 --> 12:21.480
Which basically says, accept whatever state is currently in my working directory and finalize the merge.

12:21.660 --> 12:22.080
Exactly.

12:22.500 --> 12:26.540
But by doing that, they silently accept the upstream modification.

12:26.980 --> 12:32.780
They accidentally restore all those unwanted AWS files right back into the Azure fork.

12:32.880 --> 12:33.380
Oh no.

12:33.920 --> 12:36.440
Completely undoing the strip they worked so hard to maintain.

12:36.680 --> 12:37.060
Yes.

12:37.840 --> 12:41.760
Suddenly, the Azure repository is bloated with AWS code again.

12:41.760 --> 12:46.200
And what's worse, this isn't just a one-time pain, is it? Because the upstream project keeps moving.

12:46.580 --> 12:49.760
That is the truly devious part of the modify-delete loop.

12:50.180 --> 12:54.220
Next week, upstream modifies a different file in the AWS directory.

12:54.900 --> 12:56.760
Or maybe they modify the same file again.

12:56.880 --> 12:58.120
Oh, I see where this is going.

12:58.320 --> 13:02.080
Because you previously resolved the conflict by either keeping it or deleting it again,

13:02.080 --> 13:04.420
the Git history is now hopelessly tangled.

13:05.120 --> 13:13.420
Every single new modification to any previously deleted file triggers this exact same unanswerable conflict on every single daily sync.

13:13.540 --> 13:15.640
It becomes an infinite loop of merge conflicts.

13:15.980 --> 13:17.500
The automation completely breaks down.

13:17.820 --> 13:21.480
So if I'm the architect looking at this burning pipeline, I have to ask,

13:22.300 --> 13:24.900
how do you fix an unresolvable merge conflict?

13:24.900 --> 13:30.600
And the team's answer, which I found incredibly elegant, was, you stop merging entirely.

13:31.000 --> 13:33.000
This was their big aha moment.

13:33.380 --> 13:38.060
They stepped back and looked at the branch that was failing the one tracking upstream, which they call fork upstream.

13:38.400 --> 13:38.620
Okay.

13:38.800 --> 13:40.320
They realized a fundamental truth.

13:41.080 --> 13:44.320
A human engineer never actually needs to edit this branch.

13:44.400 --> 13:46.420
Right. It's just a landing zone for incoming code.

13:46.480 --> 13:46.800
Exactly.

13:47.020 --> 13:51.480
It is written only by automation, and it is read only by their downstream integration branches.

13:51.840 --> 13:54.760
What's fascinating here is the conceptual shift.

13:54.900 --> 13:57.100
They reframed the problem entirely.

13:57.320 --> 13:57.800
How so?

13:58.000 --> 14:03.200
If nobody's ever committing handwritten code to it, it doesn't need to be a standard Git merge.

14:03.360 --> 14:05.880
It doesn't need to reconcile two divergent histories.

14:06.220 --> 14:07.960
It can be a generated tree.

14:08.260 --> 14:09.160
A generated tree.

14:09.440 --> 14:09.600
Okay.

14:09.660 --> 14:12.920
Let's unpack how they actually achieved that without using Git merge,

14:13.040 --> 14:16.340
because they had to dive deep into Git plumbing to pull this off, didn't they?

14:16.440 --> 14:17.240
They did.

14:17.700 --> 14:20.980
Most developers only ever use Git porcelain commands.

14:20.980 --> 14:25.520
You know, the user-friendly commands like Git pull, Git commit, Git merge.

14:25.620 --> 14:26.980
Right. The stuff we use every day.

14:27.100 --> 14:32.120
But underneath, Git is just a directed acyclic graph, a database of objects.

14:32.360 --> 14:37.300
The team bypassed the porcelain entirely and used low-level Git plumbing commands.

14:37.300 --> 14:41.160
So how does the new sync process actually work step by step?

14:41.360 --> 14:43.400
Every night, the automation wakes up.

14:43.740 --> 14:46.480
It reads the absolute tip of the upstream repository.

14:46.780 --> 14:52.680
It pulls that raw code into a temporary isolated directory outside of the normal Git working tree.

14:52.780 --> 14:53.940
So it's not even in the repo yet.

14:54.140 --> 14:54.340
Right.

14:54.920 --> 14:57.240
Then it runs a custom filter script.

14:57.240 --> 15:04.560
This script aggressively deletes the AWS provider, the Google Cloud provider, IBM, anything that isn't core logic.

15:04.780 --> 15:09.000
So it cleans the code from scratch every single time, totally outside of Git's awareness.

15:09.180 --> 15:09.400
Yes.

15:09.680 --> 15:18.500
Once the code is perfectly filtered, the automation uses a plumbing command called git read free to load this new pristine state directly into Git staging area.

15:18.620 --> 15:19.180
Okay. Wow.

15:19.260 --> 15:23.000
Then it uses git commit tree to write that staging area as a brand new commit.

15:23.320 --> 15:25.360
And here is the truly clever part.

15:25.360 --> 15:31.460
When they write that new commit, they artificially assign it to parents in the Git history.

15:31.660 --> 15:31.960
Yes.

15:32.140 --> 15:38.740
They tell Git, the parent of this commit is yesterday's fork upstream commit and d the upstream commit we just downloaded.

15:39.040 --> 15:39.540
Precisely.

15:39.720 --> 15:42.780
To anyone looking at the Git graph, it looks exactly like a merge.

15:43.240 --> 15:46.660
It acts like a merge for tracking provenance and history.

15:47.340 --> 15:53.860
But the Git merge algorithm itself, the engine that compares files and generates conflicts, never actually runs.

15:53.860 --> 15:58.340
It just slaps the heavily filtered tree directly onto the timeline is absolute truth.

15:58.540 --> 15:58.900
Exactly.

15:59.440 --> 16:04.940
The modified elite conflict is physically impossible because the system never attempts to reconcile the two states.

16:05.100 --> 16:06.660
It just overwrites reality.

16:06.880 --> 16:10.360
That is an incredible use of Git plumbing to solve a process problem.

16:10.660 --> 16:13.520
But I have to push back a little here or at least play devil's advocate.

16:13.680 --> 16:13.840
Okay.

16:13.940 --> 16:14.320
Go for it.

16:14.320 --> 16:23.420
If we are aggressively filtering this massive incoming wave of code every single day based on a script, what happens when upstream invents a completely new folder?

16:23.420 --> 16:27.460
Say they add a new cloud provider or a new shared authentication module.

16:28.000 --> 16:30.620
And our filter script has never seen that folder name before.

16:30.620 --> 16:35.500
That is the exact edge case that makes or breaks an automated system at this scale.

16:35.840 --> 16:38.840
If your filter encounters an unknown entity, what do you do?

16:38.920 --> 16:40.460
Do you keep it or do you delete it?

16:40.920 --> 16:48.840
Because if I default to keep, I might accidentally suck in an entirely new AWS implementation that bloats the repo.

16:49.200 --> 16:57.980
But if I default to drop, I might silently delete a critical new shared module that the core logic desperately needs to compile and the whole build breaks.

16:57.980 --> 17:02.380
And silent failures are the absolute enemy of engineering at this scale.

17:02.900 --> 17:06.740
Their solution is a strict architectural principle documented in the ADRs.

17:07.040 --> 17:08.560
They call it halt on the unknown.

17:08.780 --> 17:09.380
Halt on the unknown.

17:09.580 --> 17:09.740
Right.

17:10.140 --> 17:14.540
The filter engine is designed to exhaustively classify everything at the root level of the repository.

17:15.280 --> 17:22.540
If it encounters a top level directory, a testing module, or a build profile, it does not explicitly recognize as either keep or drop.

17:22.740 --> 17:23.680
It does not try to guess.

17:23.740 --> 17:24.280
It just crashes.

17:24.500 --> 17:25.780
It gracefully crashes.

17:25.780 --> 17:28.860
It immediately exits with a non-zero exit code.

17:28.980 --> 17:35.620
Usually code 2 halts the synchronization run and automatically opens a high-priority ticket for a human engineer to review.

17:36.300 --> 17:37.340
That makes total sense.

17:37.700 --> 17:43.220
Because guessing might be convenient exactly one time, but it will be wrong forever after.

17:43.400 --> 17:43.860
Exactly.

17:44.040 --> 17:53.940
By forcing a loud failure over a quiet one, they force the engineering team to explicitly update the filter script to account for the new architectural reality of the upstream project.

17:53.940 --> 17:55.820
It forces continuous alignment.

17:56.320 --> 18:04.780
So now that we understand how they safely pull this code in without destroying the CI pipeline with merge conflicts, we have to look at how the repository itself is structured.

18:04.780 --> 18:05.260
Right.

18:05.260 --> 18:13.040
Because managing this hybrid ownership where upstream owns the core and Azure owns the provider requires a very specific branching strategy.

18:13.300 --> 18:13.480
Right.

18:13.700 --> 18:15.960
And they aren't just managing one single repository.

18:16.300 --> 18:20.180
As a service owner, you are managing one of eight core OSDU services.

18:20.660 --> 18:24.120
Storage, schema, search, entitlements, legal, and so on.

18:24.120 --> 18:27.240
And across all of them, they enforce a strict three-branch design.

18:27.620 --> 18:27.900
Yes.

18:28.160 --> 18:32.500
The three branches are fork upstream, fork integration, and main.

18:33.260 --> 18:35.140
And they are not interchangeable.

18:35.260 --> 18:38.780
They serve very distinct purposes in the lifecycle of the code.

18:38.860 --> 18:39.100
Okay.

18:39.200 --> 18:40.520
So we just talked about fork upstream.

18:40.880 --> 18:43.100
That is the generated filtered tree.

18:43.800 --> 18:48.500
Crucially, if I'm an Azure engineer, I am never writing code on this branch.

18:48.680 --> 18:49.000
Never.

18:49.000 --> 18:56.420
In fact, there's absolutely no Azure source code living on this branch at all because the filter stripped the providers.

18:57.240 --> 18:58.780
It is providerless by construction.

18:59.060 --> 18:59.200
Right.

18:59.320 --> 19:00.460
Then you have fork integration.

19:00.840 --> 19:01.540
This is the workspace.

19:01.780 --> 19:02.680
This is the melting pot.

19:02.780 --> 19:03.340
The melting pot.

19:03.420 --> 19:03.820
I like that.

19:03.900 --> 19:09.160
This is where the generated upstream code from fork upstream is actually merged with the Azure-owned code.

19:09.580 --> 19:13.820
It's where the versions are stamped, the builds are run, and critically, where things are allowed to break.

19:14.140 --> 19:17.180
And finally, you have main, the protected production branch.

19:17.180 --> 19:21.200
Code only gets a main if it has been validated in the integration branch.

19:21.800 --> 19:23.200
But why three?

19:23.680 --> 19:24.100
What do you mean?

19:24.440 --> 19:29.080
If I'm trying to keep things simple, why wouldn't I just have fork upstream merge directly into main?

19:29.080 --> 19:37.460
If we connect this to the bigger picture, architectural decision record 001 explicitly rejected using just one or two branches.

19:38.100 --> 19:38.340
Why?

19:38.860 --> 19:46.080
Because you never want to put conflict resolution and build failures on the exact same branch you are trying to ship production code from.

19:46.080 --> 19:46.420
Ah.

19:46.960 --> 19:55.720
Because if upstream merges a change that breaks the Azure provider code and that merge happens directly on main, main is now broken.

19:55.860 --> 19:56.320
Exactly.

19:56.660 --> 20:03.780
Any hot fixes or feature work that other Azure engineers are trying to release are completely blocked until that upstream break is resolved.

20:03.880 --> 20:04.260
Exactly.

20:04.260 --> 20:11.140
By isolating integration into its own branch, if an upstream change breaks the build, that break is contained to fork integration.

20:11.860 --> 20:16.120
The team can take their time fixing it, while main remains pristine and deployable.

20:16.420 --> 20:19.080
Okay, so that handles the flow of code within a single service.

20:19.560 --> 20:21.280
But you mentioned there are eight of these services.

20:21.640 --> 20:28.940
If I'm the lead architect, I do not want to manually configure the CICD pipelines, the Git actions, and the branching rules for eight different repositories.

20:28.980 --> 20:30.100
No, you definitely don't.

20:30.140 --> 20:31.460
How do they keep them all synchronized?

20:31.460 --> 20:34.020
They use the template repository pattern.

20:34.680 --> 20:39.120
The repository OSDU-SPY, the main engineering system, is actually a template.

20:39.800 --> 20:46.020
All the other services, like OSDU-SPY Storage or OSDU-SPY Schema, are generated forks of this central template.

20:46.200 --> 20:48.580
I always like the franchise restaurant analogy for this.

20:48.940 --> 20:50.980
The temple repository is the corporate headquarters.

20:51.460 --> 20:56.420
They own the master recipe book, the CI pipelines, the automation scripts, the security checks.

20:56.520 --> 20:56.760
Right.

20:56.760 --> 21:03.880
If corporate discovers a more efficient way to fry a potato, they don't want to call every single franchise and hope they write it down correctly.

21:04.100 --> 21:04.420
No.

21:04.680 --> 21:14.600
Corporate updates the master recipe book once, and then an automated workflow takes that update and delivers a new instruction manual to every single franchise location.

21:14.600 --> 21:22.920
In this case, the automated workflow literally opens a pull request against all eight service repositories with the updated GitHub Actions YAML files.

21:23.400 --> 21:25.240
The service owners can review it and merge it.

21:25.640 --> 21:27.160
It enforces global consistency.

21:27.160 --> 21:33.160
And this pattern reveals a very strict rule they follow, which they call split what fails differently.

21:33.480 --> 21:34.580
Split what fails differently.

21:34.700 --> 21:35.260
What does that mean?

21:35.600 --> 21:41.560
Well, if you look at the template repository, there are two distinct workflow directories for GitHub Actions.

21:41.940 --> 21:46.440
One is .github workflows, and the other is .github template workflows.

21:46.720 --> 21:47.980
Why the physical separation?

21:47.980 --> 21:58.160
Because of the template repository's own internal CI testing, its own scripts failing, is a fundamentally different event than a downstream fork failing to synchronize.

21:58.240 --> 21:58.720
Oh, I see.

21:58.900 --> 22:05.820
If you mix them together, a failure in a service fork might accidentally trigger a failure in the template, masking the root cause.

22:06.740 --> 22:10.820
They physically separated the code so that one failure mode cannot bleed into the other.

22:11.060 --> 22:13.760
That level of isolation is just smart engineering.

22:13.760 --> 22:21.200
Now, before we move away from the code and into the infrastructure, there is one gaping hole in this narrative that we have to address.

22:21.300 --> 22:22.480
I think I know what you're going to ask.

22:22.700 --> 22:25.560
We established that Upstream is deleting the Azure code.

22:26.100 --> 22:29.000
The fork Upstream branch filters out all providers.

22:29.460 --> 22:31.740
So how does Azure get its code in the first place?

22:31.820 --> 22:35.320
If it's being deleted, where does the Azure provider code come from?

22:35.580 --> 22:39.840
This is a fantastic question, and it highlights a process they call the one-time seed.

22:39.840 --> 22:50.800
Because Upstream is deleting the code, the initialization of a new service fork performs an operation that happens exactly once in the lifetime of the repository and can never be repeated.

22:51.020 --> 22:51.860
It's a rescue mission.

22:52.020 --> 22:53.580
It is literally a rescue mission.

22:54.000 --> 23:05.420
The initialization script finds the absolute newest Upstream Git revision that still contains the provider partition Azure directory right before the commit where the community deleted it.

23:05.420 --> 23:15.580
It performs a textual merge to grab that directory, pulls it into the fork integration branch, and from that very second, that directory is permanently Azure property.

23:15.840 --> 23:21.540
It is exactly like Indiana Jones sliding under the stone door and grabbing his hat right before the temple collapses.

23:21.780 --> 23:22.260
It is.

23:22.660 --> 23:27.680
But this rescue mission comes with a very harsh reality, which is documented right in the ADRs.

23:27.680 --> 23:32.160
From that day forward, Upstream fixes to the Azure code do not arrive automatically.

23:32.560 --> 23:33.200
Think about it.

23:33.600 --> 23:38.440
The automated daily sync pulls from fork upstream, which we know strips the providers out.

23:38.800 --> 23:42.940
And the sync script is strictly forbidden from touching the directories owned by Azure.

23:43.120 --> 23:53.740
So if the community fixes a critical bug in the Azure provider code just hours before they delete it upstream, the Azure automated sync will completely ignore that fix.

23:54.160 --> 23:56.840
Because the automation is blind to that directory.

23:56.840 --> 24:07.700
So if a bug fix happens upstream, an Azure engineer has to manually read the upstream commit, figure out what changed, and manually copy, paste, or port that fix into the Azure repository.

24:08.000 --> 24:12.700
Brutal, but absolutely necessary to maintain the integrity of the split ownership model.

24:13.120 --> 24:17.660
You cannot automate the synchronization of a directory you claim sole ownership of.

24:17.740 --> 24:18.220
Exactly.

24:18.220 --> 24:18.460
Okay.

24:18.860 --> 24:21.600
So at this point, the engineering system has done its job.

24:21.960 --> 24:23.820
We have successfully maintained a living fork.

24:23.960 --> 24:25.620
We have survived the modified elite loop.

24:25.880 --> 24:32.100
We have our pristine built container image containing our Azure code merged perfectly with the upstream core code.

24:32.320 --> 24:32.420
Yeah.

24:32.820 --> 24:36.620
But a container image sitting in a registry is just a very expensive paperweight.

24:36.780 --> 24:38.520
You need a cluster to prove it actually works.

24:38.960 --> 24:39.440
Exactly.

24:39.440 --> 24:46.080
You cannot prove a distributed OSDU service is functional with a simple unit test on a developer's laptop.

24:46.660 --> 24:54.260
You need a real database, a real message best, real identity systems, and all the other core services running alongside it.

24:54.300 --> 24:59.020
Which brings us to the Stack Ausdus B-Stack, the deployment platform.

24:59.180 --> 24:59.420
Right.

24:59.720 --> 25:05.660
Once Azure takes permanent ownership of the provider code, they assume the responsibility of proving it works.

25:05.840 --> 25:06.740
And what does that entail?

25:06.740 --> 25:13.800
The Stack's entire purpose is to provision a massive, highly complex Azure environment from scratch in about 50 minutes,

25:13.960 --> 25:18.380
so that the GitHub actions can deploy the image and run integration tests against it.

25:18.460 --> 25:24.340
And the first major architectural decision they make for the Stack is what the documentation calls the Azure-only bet.

25:24.840 --> 25:25.900
This is a crucial pivot.

25:26.420 --> 25:32.240
When you look at the OSDU community, they spend a lot of time building deployment tools that are cloud-agnostic.

25:32.380 --> 25:36.060
Cool as they can spin up databases inside Kubernetes so you can run the whole Stack anywhere.

25:36.060 --> 25:44.600
But the Azure SPI team looked at all the stateful data services OSDU needs, databases, blob storage, event buses, and they made a hard rule.

25:45.260 --> 25:48.420
If Azure has a managed POS equivalent, we are using it.

25:48.940 --> 25:55.080
So instead of running a fragile MongoDB instance inside Kubernetes, OSDU records go to Cosmos DB.

25:55.080 --> 25:58.860
Entitlements go to Cosmos DB using the Gremlin API for graph queries.

25:59.340 --> 26:00.980
Blobs go straight to Azure storage.

26:01.460 --> 26:03.080
Events go to Azure service bus.

26:03.240 --> 26:03.580
Yes.

26:03.780 --> 26:08.660
And they explicitly state in the ADRs, portability is completely out of scope.

26:08.980 --> 26:11.280
That is a bold thing to write in an open source project.

26:11.440 --> 26:13.260
It is, but it clarifies the mission.

26:13.260 --> 26:17.240
And the phrase they use is, moving to AWS is a fork, not a flag.

26:17.580 --> 26:21.920
Meaning they are not going to clutter their deployment scripts with, if Oz then do this, else do that.

26:22.100 --> 26:24.600
They are not trying to build a generic deployment tool.

26:24.680 --> 26:26.320
They are building an Azure testing substrate.

26:26.320 --> 26:31.920
And by offloading the state to Pias, they reduce the operational burden on the Kubernetes cluster massively.

26:31.940 --> 26:32.300
Well, absolutely.

26:32.800 --> 26:43.080
Because if you are spinning up and tearing down environments constantly for testing, waiting for a massive distributed database to initialize inside Kubernetes takes forever and fails often.

26:43.240 --> 26:43.460
Oh.

26:43.700 --> 26:46.800
With Cosmos DB, the state lives outside the cluster.

26:47.000 --> 26:47.360
Exactly.

26:47.680 --> 26:55.680
You could literally delete the entire Kubernetes cluster, provision a new one, and your data survives because it was never inside the pods to begin with.

26:55.680 --> 26:56.400
Precisely.

26:56.900 --> 26:58.520
But they couldn't push everything out.

26:58.940 --> 27:01.920
Some stateful systems had to stay inside the Kubernetes cluster.

27:02.000 --> 27:02.360
Why?

27:02.460 --> 27:06.240
If the rule is use managed services, why did anything stay behind?

27:06.560 --> 27:08.920
Sometimes you hit a hard technical constraint.

27:09.200 --> 27:11.260
Elasticsearch, for example, stays in the cluster.

27:11.460 --> 27:13.000
Why not use Azure AI Search?

27:13.280 --> 27:20.280
Because Azure AI Search is not API compatible with the highly specific queries the OSDU search service expects.

27:20.460 --> 27:21.340
That's not a preference.

27:21.420 --> 27:22.060
It's a blocker.

27:22.060 --> 27:29.480
They have to run a three-node Elasticsearch cluster with 128 gigabytes of storage directly inside Kubernetes.

27:29.640 --> 27:30.760
Wow, that is a heavy footprint.

27:31.040 --> 27:31.380
It is.

27:31.520 --> 27:35.160
Then there's Redis and Postgresful, which is required for Apache Airflow.

27:35.280 --> 27:36.620
They kept those in cluster as well.

27:36.860 --> 27:40.240
But Azure has managed Redis and managed Postgresful.

27:40.460 --> 27:41.220
Why not use them?

27:41.380 --> 27:41.760
Cost.

27:41.760 --> 27:52.600
If you are spinning up dozens of ephemeral development environments every week, provisioning managed instances for Redis and Postgres every single time would absolutely bankrupt the development budget.

27:52.960 --> 27:56.500
It is cheaper and faster to run them ephemerally in cluster for testing.

27:56.980 --> 27:59.820
Okay, so the state is split between Pias and the cluster.

28:00.080 --> 28:01.700
Who is actually running this cluster?

28:02.200 --> 28:07.140
Because managing Kubernetes nodes, scaling them, and dealing with networking is a full-time job.

28:07.140 --> 28:09.140
They are using AKS Automatic.

28:09.380 --> 28:13.240
This means they are letting Azure handle the really tedious Kubernetes chores.

28:13.960 --> 28:21.880
AKS Automatic handles node provisioning dynamically using Carpenter, so it spins up exactly the right size virtual machine for the workload in seconds.

28:22.060 --> 28:22.260
Nice.

28:22.560 --> 28:27.420
It manages the Istio service mesh for them, and it enforces strict deployment safeguards.

28:27.420 --> 28:34.640
Let's talk about those deployment safeguards, because they actually forced the team into a very interesting decision regarding Helm charts.

28:35.160 --> 28:37.600
Helm is basically the package manager for Kubernetes.

28:38.020 --> 28:43.460
Usually if you want to deploy a community service, you just download the upstream Helm chart and run it.

28:43.580 --> 28:46.320
But the SPI team rejected the upstream charts.

28:46.560 --> 28:47.120
They had to.

28:47.700 --> 28:50.620
AKS Automatic enforces strict security profiles.

28:50.620 --> 28:58.700
It uses Azure policy to block any container that tries to run as a root user or demands elevated Linux capabilities like Capnet admin.

28:59.180 --> 29:00.540
Which is standard security best practice.

29:00.760 --> 29:01.240
It is.

29:01.720 --> 29:09.100
But if you rely on an upstream community Helm chart, the community might bump a version and accidentally drop the run as non-root.

29:09.540 --> 29:10.020
True flag.

29:10.160 --> 29:10.440
Ah.

29:10.720 --> 29:12.780
And if they drop that flag, what happens?

29:12.920 --> 29:15.220
The AKS safeguards will instantly reject the deployment.

29:15.220 --> 29:21.660
The pod will never start, and you are left debugging a cryptic Kubernetes admission error, trying to figure out why the pipeline failed.

29:22.080 --> 29:28.060
To prevent this, the stack team maintains one single, locally controlled Helm chart for all OSDU services.

29:28.220 --> 29:29.840
So they centralize the deployment logic.

29:30.260 --> 29:30.740
Exactly.

29:30.980 --> 29:34.840
They bake all the strict security compliance directly into that one chart.

29:35.160 --> 29:43.680
It guarantees that every single service drops all capabilities and runs as a non-root user, rendering the deployments immune to upstream chart drift.

29:43.680 --> 29:46.700
So we have this architecture that is heavily tied to Azure.

29:47.060 --> 29:55.180
We have 50 different resources to provision virtual networks, NAT gateways, key vaults, service buzz topics, Cosmos DB accounts.

29:55.660 --> 30:03.020
If I'm an engineer looking to automate this, my first thought is Terraform, or maybe just a giant Python script that calls the Azure CLI.

30:03.320 --> 30:04.940
How do they solve the orchestration problem?

30:05.220 --> 30:10.580
The division of labor here is very deliberate, and they rejected both of those initial ideas for very specific reasons.

30:10.720 --> 30:11.660
Let's start with Terraform.

30:11.880 --> 30:12.980
Why reject Terraform?

30:12.980 --> 30:15.700
It's the industry standard for infrastructure as code.

30:16.020 --> 30:20.160
Terraform is fantastic, but it requires maintaining statefiles.tfstate.

30:20.600 --> 30:29.120
In a fast-moving, highly automated development platform where CI pipelines are constantly spinning up and tearing down environments, dealing with state file locking is a nightmare.

30:29.460 --> 30:29.640
Right.

30:29.640 --> 30:37.600
If a CI runner gets killed due to an out-of-memory error while it holds the lock on the state file in a storage blob, the state is locked forever.

30:38.340 --> 30:39.640
The next pipeline run fails.

30:40.280 --> 30:43.760
You have to manually go in, break the lock, and hope the state isn't corrupted.

30:43.940 --> 30:44.320
Exactly.

30:44.320 --> 30:47.240
It's an operational headache they explicitly didn't want.

30:47.680 --> 30:50.940
So then you might say, what about a pure CLI orchestrator?

30:51.320 --> 30:56.140
Just a massive Python script calling as resource, create sequentially.

30:56.140 --> 30:58.340
But a Python script is imperative.

30:58.660 --> 31:00.020
It just executes commands.

31:00.400 --> 31:02.000
It lacks preview capabilities.

31:02.120 --> 31:05.820
You can't run a what-if command to see what the script is going to change before it actually does it.

31:05.900 --> 31:06.140
Right.

31:06.400 --> 31:12.800
And maintaining the dependency ordering, making sure the network exists before the database, is incredibly brittle in a pure script.

31:12.800 --> 31:14.360
So they split the problem in half.

31:14.840 --> 31:18.680
To provision the Azure resources, they use handwritten BICEP templates.

31:19.000 --> 31:22.320
BICEP being Microsoft's declarative language for Azure resources.

31:22.480 --> 31:22.740
Yes.

31:23.060 --> 31:24.300
BICEP is stateless.

31:24.700 --> 31:28.440
It queries the actual Azure control plane directly to figure out what needs to change.

31:28.720 --> 31:29.780
It integrates natively.

31:29.900 --> 31:31.600
It handles dependency graphs automatically.

31:31.860 --> 31:33.700
And it gives you a what-if dry run.

31:33.960 --> 31:34.180
Okay.

31:34.280 --> 31:35.740
So BICEP handles the cloud.

31:36.220 --> 31:37.560
What handles Kubernetes?

31:38.040 --> 31:40.040
For the Kubernetes side, they use Flux.

31:40.280 --> 31:41.780
Flux is a GitOps controller.

31:41.780 --> 31:43.020
I love GitOps.

31:43.420 --> 31:45.900
Explain how Flux changes the deployment paradigm.

31:46.080 --> 31:49.340
Normally, your CI pipeline pushes code into the cluster.

31:49.680 --> 31:50.660
That's a push model.

31:51.200 --> 31:52.100
Flux is a pull model.

31:52.580 --> 31:54.640
Flux lives inside the Kubernetes cluster.

31:54.900 --> 31:57.060
It constantly watches a specific Git repository.

31:57.620 --> 32:01.260
And it forces the cluster to match the manifest declared in that repository.

32:01.920 --> 32:03.760
It's an endless reconciliation loop.

32:04.040 --> 32:11.340
So if I manually go into the cluster and delete a deployment, Flux sees the drift from the Git repo and instantly recreates it.

32:11.340 --> 32:11.780
Exactly.

32:12.500 --> 32:13.760
So the workflow is this.

32:14.080 --> 32:17.540
The Python CLI, a command called spyup, is just a thin wrapper.

32:18.020 --> 32:20.080
It kicks off the BICEP templates to build the cloud.

32:20.280 --> 32:22.100
It sets up a few necessary configurations.

32:22.460 --> 32:25.480
It installs Flux into the cluster, points Flux at the repository.

32:25.920 --> 32:27.960
And then the CLI gets completely out of the way.

32:28.260 --> 32:30.320
Flux takes over and hydrates the cluster.

32:30.460 --> 32:33.440
Which brings us to a part of this deep dive that absolutely blew my mind.

32:33.440 --> 32:39.160
When you are deploying 50 resources and dozens of microservices, they all have to talk to each other.

32:39.440 --> 32:45.680
Normally, that means generating passwords, connection strings, and API keys and storing them in Azure Key Vault.

32:46.320 --> 32:49.480
But the SPI team made a radical security decision.

32:50.560 --> 32:55.380
How do you secure a massive distributed system without creating secrets that can leak?

32:55.720 --> 32:57.160
You turn the keys off entirely.

32:57.360 --> 32:58.200
It sounds impossible.

32:58.340 --> 32:58.800
Explain this.

32:58.800 --> 33:00.740
It is a profound security posture.

33:01.100 --> 33:08.260
When they write the BICEP templates, they explicitly disable local authentication for Cosmos DB, Service Bus, and Storage.

33:08.840 --> 33:14.640
Disabling local authentication means you literally cannot generate a password or a connection string for that database.

33:15.080 --> 33:17.580
The feature is turned off at the Azure control plane level.

33:17.760 --> 33:18.240
Exactly.

33:18.580 --> 33:20.720
This means there are no connection strings.

33:21.100 --> 33:23.640
There is no password to store in Azure Key Vault.

33:23.640 --> 33:28.300
There is no secret for a developer to accidentally print to a log file or commit to GitHub.

33:28.840 --> 33:31.180
A storm credential is a credential that can be stolen.

33:31.700 --> 33:35.380
By not having them, they eliminate an entire class of security vulnerabilities.

33:35.680 --> 33:43.180
But if there are no passwords, how does the storage service inside Kubernetes prove to Cosmos DB that it's allowed to read the data?

33:43.380 --> 33:45.280
They use Azure Workload Identity.

33:45.540 --> 33:48.040
It relies on a concept called identity federation.

33:48.040 --> 33:58.220
When a Kubernetes pod starts up, the cluster injects a short-lived, projected EnterID JSON web token, a JWT, directly into the pod's file system.

33:58.340 --> 33:59.620
Okay, so the pod has a token.

33:59.840 --> 34:02.740
But this token isn't a static secret you can easily copy and paste.

34:02.960 --> 34:11.060
It's a cryptographic assertion cryptographically bound to that specific pod's service account, and it is rotated automatically by Kubernetes every few hours.

34:11.500 --> 34:15.540
When the pod wants to talk to Cosmos DB, it presents this token.

34:15.540 --> 34:26.320
And Cosmos DB looks at the token, verifies the cryptographic signature with Microsoft's EnterID, checks its internal role assignments to see if that identity has data contributor access, and lets it in.

34:26.720 --> 34:27.800
No passwords required.

34:27.980 --> 34:28.360
Exactly.

34:28.780 --> 34:31.160
That handles how the internal services talk to Azure.

34:31.400 --> 34:32.880
But that is only half the battle.

34:33.340 --> 34:34.740
What about inbound requests?

34:35.000 --> 34:40.400
How does a user or a downstream service prove who they are when calling an OSDU API from the outside?

34:40.400 --> 34:45.260
Right, because OSDU was built years ago by the community, and it has some legacy baggage.

34:46.180 --> 34:57.240
The documentation mentions that the Azure provider code in OSDU expects legacy HTTP headers for authorization, specifically headers called xappied and xusered.

34:57.360 --> 34:57.640
Right.

34:57.940 --> 35:04.160
It expects these because it was originally built around an older Spring Boot filter chain that looked for those exact strings.

35:04.160 --> 35:07.680
And this raises a massive red flag for any security architect.

35:08.260 --> 35:15.500
If a service blindly trusts an HTTP header for authorization, what stops a malicious actor from just smoothing that header?

35:15.700 --> 35:26.040
If I open my terminal right now and use curl to send a request to the API, and I just manually add the header xuserade superadmin, does the legacy application code just grant me admin access?

35:26.220 --> 35:28.660
If the architecture were naive, yes, it absolutely would.

35:28.720 --> 35:30.740
The application would read the header and trust it.

35:30.740 --> 35:34.080
So how do they prevent that without rewriting the entire legacy application code?

35:34.200 --> 35:38.640
The solution here is incredibly elegant, and it relies on the Istio service mesh.

35:38.920 --> 35:40.720
Istio operates using a sidecar pattern.

35:41.360 --> 35:47.620
They deployed an Envoy proxy filter container into the exact same pod as every single OSDU application container.

35:48.020 --> 35:49.800
So the proxy sits right next to the app.

35:49.900 --> 35:54.420
Right, and this proxy intercepts all network traffic before it even reaches the application's port.

35:54.420 --> 36:06.580
When a request comes in from the outside, the Envoy filter completely strips away any incoming xappad or xuserade headers that a user might have maliciously tried to inject.

36:06.740 --> 36:08.220
It sanitizes the request.

36:08.380 --> 36:12.660
Okay, so the spoofed headers are gone, but the application still needs those headers to function.

36:13.060 --> 36:13.840
Where do they come from?

36:14.180 --> 36:19.160
The user is required to provide a valid JSON web token in the authorization header.

36:19.160 --> 36:23.440
The Envoy filter validates the cryptographic signature of that JWT.

36:23.780 --> 36:29.360
If the token is valid, the Envoy filter extracts the trusted cryptographic claims from inside that token,

36:29.560 --> 36:37.260
and the proxy itself repopulates the xuserade and xappad headers before passing the request to the application container over a local host.

36:37.440 --> 36:38.180
That is brilliant.

36:38.340 --> 36:42.800
The legacy application code gets the exact headers it expects, so it doesn't need to be rewritten.

36:42.800 --> 36:48.880
But those headers are guaranteed to have been cryptographically verified and injected by the infrastructure, not the user.

36:49.220 --> 36:51.560
It completely neutralizes header spoofing.

36:51.960 --> 36:56.980
It's a perfect example of infrastructure bending to accommodate the realities of legacy code securely.

36:57.620 --> 36:59.880
But the complexity doesn't stop there.

37:00.240 --> 37:05.740
Because when you are mapping claims from a token to a header, the exact name of the claim matters.

37:06.180 --> 37:11.520
And I noticed a quirky detail in the documentation about V1 versus V2 enter ID token.

37:11.520 --> 37:13.720
Yes. I saw that too. What is that about?

37:14.020 --> 37:17.140
It's a fascinating little detail about the evolution of identity standards.

37:17.780 --> 37:25.000
When the Envoy filter maps the claims to the headers, the xappad header is supposed to be populated from the token's application identifier claim.

37:25.540 --> 37:30.180
In an older V1 enter ID token, that claim is literally called APID.

37:30.640 --> 37:31.160
Makes sense.

37:31.300 --> 37:33.200
But Microsoft updated their standards.

37:33.200 --> 37:41.780
In a newer V2 token aligning with OpenID Connect specs, Microsoft changed the claim name from APID to ASP, which stands for Authorized Party.

37:42.360 --> 37:48.040
So if a user sends a V2 token, the Envoy filter looks for APID, doesn't find it, and the mapping silently fails.

37:48.300 --> 37:51.380
The legacy application gets an empty header and denies access.

37:51.380 --> 37:57.180
Precisely. And this explains a seemingly bizarre choice in the SPI team's CLI tooling.

37:57.840 --> 38:06.360
When their CLI script mints a test token for developers to use during local debugging, it deliberately hits the legacy V1 Microsoft authentication endpoint.

38:06.940 --> 38:15.440
It does this solely so the resulting token contains the APID claim, ensuring the Envoy filter mapping works exactly as the legacy OSDU code expects.

38:15.440 --> 38:24.300
It's those tiny cascading dependencies from a community standard change down to a proxy mapping rule that makes system architecture so wild.

38:25.200 --> 38:28.280
Okay, I have to pause and push back on one thing regarding identity, though.

38:28.440 --> 38:32.940
Because as much as I love the Envoy filter, I noticed something in the ADRs that alarmed me.

38:33.120 --> 38:33.520
What's that?

38:33.680 --> 38:38.340
The documentation mentions that all these different OSDU services search, storage, schema shortages,

38:38.660 --> 38:43.260
they all share a single, identical managed identity for accessing Azure resources.

38:43.260 --> 38:48.820
Wait, if one managed identity is shared by all the services, haven't they just created a massive security crater?

38:49.000 --> 38:49.640
How do you figure?

38:50.000 --> 38:57.140
If a hacker compromises the SERP service, that pod has the exact same identity and therefore the exact same permissions as the entitlement service.

38:57.500 --> 38:59.120
There is no blast radius containment.

38:59.460 --> 39:05.240
You've spotted a significant compromise, and it's the exact tradeoff they explicitly documented and accepted.

39:05.460 --> 39:06.660
Why would they accept that risk?

39:06.780 --> 39:08.040
They considered the alternative.

39:08.040 --> 39:14.280
The alternative is creating a unique, distinct, managed identity for every single microservice.

39:14.840 --> 39:27.680
But if they did that, the sheer volume of role-based access control assignments, the RBAC, and the federated credential configurations required in the BICEP templates would be an absolute nightmare to maintain and deploy.

39:27.780 --> 39:27.920
Right.

39:28.000 --> 39:32.680
You'd be deploying dozens of identities and hundreds of role assignments just to get the cluster up.

39:32.680 --> 39:33.180
Exactly.

39:33.180 --> 39:40.500
And when they looked at the actual API surface of OSDU, they realized the permissions didn't differentiate enough to justify that complexity.

39:41.340 --> 39:45.420
Almost every service needs read-write access to Cosmos DB and storage anyway.

39:45.640 --> 39:47.900
The documentation states this cost openly.

39:48.400 --> 39:52.120
This is a development and testing platform, not a production banking environment.

39:52.840 --> 39:58.920
They deliberately chose operational simplicity and deployment speed over granular service-to-service internal isolation.

39:58.920 --> 39:59.880
Fair enough.

40:00.060 --> 40:00.920
They know the risk.

40:01.040 --> 40:02.860
They documented the risk, and they accepted it.

40:03.540 --> 40:04.660
Moving on to bootstrapping.

40:04.980 --> 40:07.500
So we have our ultra-secure cluster running.

40:07.740 --> 40:09.280
The databases are provisioned.

40:09.300 --> 40:10.080
The pods are running.

40:10.220 --> 40:11.980
The envoy filters are intercepting traffic.

40:12.180 --> 40:16.860
But if you query the OSDU API right now, it returns absolutely nothing.

40:16.980 --> 40:19.460
An empty database is just a very expensive heater.

40:19.600 --> 40:19.800
Right.

40:19.800 --> 40:25.600
How does the system automatically transition from an empty state to actually being a functional data environment?

40:25.860 --> 40:28.120
Because you can't just load data randomly.

40:28.480 --> 40:29.420
There are dependencies.

40:30.120 --> 40:33.000
This is where a concept they call bootstrap as data comes in.

40:33.360 --> 40:36.980
They use one single flux-managed Helm chart called Osdu Spy Init.

40:37.600 --> 40:40.180
This chart doesn't deploy long-running application services.

40:40.660 --> 40:42.800
It deploys one-shot Kubernetes jobs.

40:43.280 --> 40:49.480
These jobs run once, execute a script to seed the initial data required for OSDU to wake up, and then terminate.

40:49.480 --> 40:50.860
What kind of data are we talking about?

40:51.120 --> 40:55.560
It creates the core partition records, which tell the system what a tenant is.

40:55.880 --> 41:01.980
It establishes the root entitlement groups in the graph database, which is required for anyone to have admin rights.

41:02.340 --> 41:10.960
And it seeds the default legal tags, because in the energy industry, you cannot ingest a piece of data unless it is tagged with a legal compliance policy.

41:11.740 --> 41:15.020
And the order of operations here must be incredibly delicate.

41:15.640 --> 41:18.440
It is a strict dependency graph enforced by flux.

41:18.440 --> 41:20.740
You can't just fire all these jobs at once.

41:20.860 --> 41:31.740
For example, you cannot load the schemas, and there are over 1,300 complex JSON schemas that define industry standards until the entitlement service is up and running and seeded with root groups.

41:31.840 --> 41:32.060
Why?

41:32.380 --> 41:39.000
Because the schema loader job has to authenticate itself against the entitlements API just to get permission to write the schemas into the database.

41:39.280 --> 41:41.040
So the dependency graph looks like this.

41:41.040 --> 41:45.300
The certificate authority bundle waits for Redis and Elasticsearch to be healthy.

41:45.740 --> 41:48.880
The core OSDU services wait for the CA bundle.

41:49.380 --> 41:53.100
The initialization jobs wait for the core services to respond to health checks.

41:53.360 --> 41:57.740
And the massive schema loader waits for the initialization jobs to finish creating the admin groups.

41:57.880 --> 41:59.440
It's a perfectly choreographed dance.

41:59.440 --> 42:04.400
But there's one fascinating exception to this strict dependency graph that I noticed in the manifests.

42:05.520 --> 42:10.600
The legal tag initialization job has a specific metadata label on it.

42:11.120 --> 42:11.400
Gating.

42:12.120 --> 42:12.380
False.

42:13.060 --> 42:16.200
Why would they explicitly ungate the legal tags?

42:16.700 --> 42:21.460
If legal tags are required to ingest data, shouldn't their failure halt the deployment?

42:21.460 --> 42:26.440
This is a very mature distinction they made between infrastructure health and application data state.

42:27.060 --> 42:29.180
A missing legal tag is a data problem.

42:29.620 --> 42:31.220
It is not a platform problem.

42:31.400 --> 42:32.060
Explain the difference.

42:32.260 --> 42:37.540
If the legal service API is up and running, the database is healthy and the networking is fine, the infrastructure is successful.

42:38.100 --> 42:43.420
If the initialization script fails to seed a specific default tag because of a typo in a JSON file,

42:43.860 --> 42:48.640
that shouldn't block the entire Kubernetes cluster from reporting that it is fundamentally ready to flex.

42:48.640 --> 42:53.380
It prevents an application-level data quirk from holding the infrastructure reporting hostage.

42:53.680 --> 42:54.600
That makes a lot of sense.

42:55.020 --> 43:00.820
You don't want a pipeline to show a red infrastructure failure when the only issue is a missing text spring in a database.

43:01.380 --> 43:05.680
Now, while all this initialization is happening, there is a fundamental mechanical question.

43:06.300 --> 43:10.720
How does the cluster actually know which specific container image to run for each service?

43:11.020 --> 43:13.100
Because remember, this is a CICD environment.

43:13.100 --> 43:18.860
The Azure SBI factory is constantly building new images with new Git commit SHAs every day.

43:19.320 --> 43:21.920
This relies on a mechanism they call the image lock.

43:22.220 --> 43:24.980
It is arguably the most important piece of state in the cluster.

43:25.660 --> 43:29.260
It is a single Kubernetes config map named Osdo image lock.

43:29.640 --> 43:32.520
It acts as the absolute source of truth for image references.

43:32.780 --> 43:33.320
What's inside it?

43:33.540 --> 43:34.920
It is a simple key value store.

43:34.920 --> 43:41.800
It holds the repository URL, the tag, and the exact cryptographic SHA-256 digest for every single service.

43:42.140 --> 43:42.940
Storage image.

43:43.140 --> 43:45.100
Shaku56.abc123.

43:45.160 --> 43:46.180
So how does Flux use that?

43:46.480 --> 43:54.660
When Flux, the GitOps controller, renders the Kubernetes manifest from the Git repository, it uses a post-build substitution step.

43:55.020 --> 43:59.620
If you look at the raw deployment manifest in Git, it doesn't contain an image tag at all.

43:59.720 --> 44:01.280
It just has a variable placeholder.

44:01.280 --> 44:10.920
Flux reads the manifest, reads the config map, and injects the exact digest found in that config map into the pod definition before applying it to the cluster.

44:11.200 --> 44:11.880
I love this.

44:12.200 --> 44:14.920
It's exactly like a master casting board at a theater.

44:15.300 --> 44:20.040
The script, which is the Kubernetes manifest in Git, just says lead actor walks on stage.

44:20.280 --> 44:21.860
The script doesn't care who the actor is.

44:22.200 --> 44:27.440
The casting board, the config map, sitting in the theater lobby is what dictates exactly who is playing that role tonight.

44:27.440 --> 44:34.100
And extending that analogy, if the lead actor gets sick and you need an understudy, you don't rewrite the script, you just update the casting board in the lobby.

44:34.520 --> 44:40.120
What's brilliant here is that changing an image doesn't require making a Git commit to modify the application manifests.

44:40.580 --> 44:50.260
You just update the config map in the cluster, and Flux instantly sees the change, realizes the running pod doesn't match the new casting board, and performs a rolling update to the new image.

44:50.260 --> 44:56.980
And this specific mechanism paves the way for the most complex part of this entire architecture, the handshake.

44:57.300 --> 44:59.300
We have finally arrived at the handshake.

44:59.560 --> 45:00.580
Okay, let's set the scene.

45:00.780 --> 45:03.400
We have our engineered code maintained in the permanent fork.

45:03.740 --> 45:06.060
The factory just built a brand new container image.

45:06.340 --> 45:11.420
We have our running Azure platform bootstrapped and secured with Envoy filters and workload identities.

45:11.420 --> 45:20.920
Now, how does a GitHub action running on a random Microsoft-hosted runner somewhere in the cloud safely test its newly built image on this live, highly secured Azure cluster?

45:21.380 --> 45:24.480
This solves what the team calls the isolated test problem.

45:24.980 --> 45:34.480
When the community ran OSDU, they kept all the testing knowledge, the API endpoints, the tenant IDs, the client secrets hidden away in their own internal private cloud pipelines.

45:34.980 --> 45:40.180
When Azure created this permanent fork and severed those pipelines, the integration tests were orphaned.

45:40.180 --> 45:41.900
The tests existed in the source code.

45:41.980 --> 45:47.320
They compiled perfectly, but nobody knew how to actually run them because the tests didn't know where to point.

45:47.500 --> 45:47.960
Exactly.

45:47.960 --> 45:55.640
The obvious answer, the way most people solve this, is just to store the cluster endpoints in GitHub secrets or variables, right?

45:55.720 --> 45:55.840
Right.

45:55.900 --> 46:02.480
You just add gateway URL is https.mycluster.azure.com into the GitHub repository settings.

46:02.660 --> 46:03.960
That approach was rejected immediately.

46:04.980 --> 46:05.200
Why?

46:05.620 --> 46:07.780
Because these environments are ephemeral.

46:07.780 --> 46:09.900
They are rebuilt weekly, sometimes daily.

46:10.120 --> 46:11.400
IP addresses change.

46:11.520 --> 46:12.800
Gateway URLs change.

46:13.220 --> 46:14.600
Tenant IDs might change.

46:14.880 --> 46:19.200
If you hardcode them in GitHub, your tests will break every single week when the infrastructure cycles.

46:19.620 --> 46:21.340
You'd spend all your time updating variables.

46:21.820 --> 46:23.840
So instead, they designed a two-sided contract.

46:24.100 --> 46:26.840
They split the knowledge into a descriptor and facts.

46:27.340 --> 46:31.160
Let's break that down because it's a beautiful pattern for decoupling CI from CD.

46:31.640 --> 46:34.320
The fork repository, the code side, owns the descriptor.

46:34.320 --> 46:38.420
This is a file called .spyservice.yamanl checked into the repo.

46:38.820 --> 46:41.060
It declares the symbolic needs of the test suite.

46:41.540 --> 46:47.240
It basically says, in order to run these tests, I need a gateway URL, I need a tenant ID, and I need a data partition.

46:47.480 --> 46:49.020
It doesn't know what the values are.

46:49.200 --> 46:50.760
It just declares what it needs.

46:50.900 --> 46:51.280
Exactly.

46:51.760 --> 46:54.480
On the other side, the platform owns the facts.

46:54.480 --> 47:03.740
The live environment can generate a JSON file via CLI command spy info.json reporting exactly what the environment actually is at that exact moment.

47:04.380 --> 47:06.420
Here is my gateway URL today.

47:06.640 --> 47:08.280
Here is my tenant ID today.

47:08.580 --> 47:13.540
And the GitHub Action Validation Lane is the mediator that joins these two sides together at runtime.

47:13.540 --> 47:23.960
It reads the symbolic needs from the descriptor, fetches the actual values from the facts by querying the cluster, and binds them together as environment variables just before running the tests.

47:24.400 --> 47:25.800
It is entirely dynamic.

47:25.800 --> 47:29.320
It completely decouples the test definitions from the infrastructure state.

47:29.840 --> 47:36.960
But to fetch those facts, and more importantly, to deploy the new image to the cluster for testing, the GitHub Action needs access to the cluster.

47:37.160 --> 47:39.060
And this is where my security alarms go off again.

47:39.060 --> 47:45.860
If the GitHub test runner needs to deploy a brand new image to the cluster, doesn't GitHub need right access to Kubernetes?

47:46.600 --> 47:52.200
If someone compromises that GitHub token or injects malicious code into a pull request, they own the cluster.

47:52.480 --> 47:56.140
It would be a terrifying risk, which is why the security boundary here is rigorous.

47:57.360 --> 47:59.780
The GitHub token does not have cluster admin rights.

48:00.120 --> 48:01.260
Then how does it do anything?

48:01.440 --> 48:03.200
It uses workload identity federation.

48:03.200 --> 48:10.000
The GitHub Action runner is only granted permission to assume a very specific lockdown deploy identity in Azure.

48:10.700 --> 48:17.100
And that deploy identity has absolutely zero kubektal delete or Kubernetes secret read access.

48:17.260 --> 48:17.600
None.

48:18.100 --> 48:19.660
Then how does it deploy the image?

48:19.900 --> 48:23.000
It relies entirely on the image lock config map we discussed earlier.

48:23.560 --> 48:30.480
The deploy identity is granted Kubernetes RBAC permissions to edit exactly one single object in the entire cluster.

48:30.480 --> 48:33.300
The OSDU image lock config map, that's it.

48:33.360 --> 48:34.520
It cannot touch anything else.

48:34.640 --> 48:36.660
Oh, it just updates the casting board.

48:36.940 --> 48:46.600
And because Flux, which lives inside the cluster and already has the necessary admin permissions, is constantly watching that casting board, Flux is the one that actually performs the pod rollout.

48:46.740 --> 48:47.140
Exactly.

48:47.400 --> 48:48.460
GitOps stays alive.

48:48.700 --> 48:51.640
The external caller GitHub just asks for a change in the lock.

48:51.820 --> 48:54.240
And the internal system Flux annexes it safely.

48:54.400 --> 48:58.980
Which brings us to the exact chronological execution of this handshake transaction.

48:58.980 --> 49:01.980
The documentation breaks this down into three phases.

49:02.220 --> 49:04.780
The borrow, the proof, and the restore.

49:05.260 --> 49:07.860
Walk me through exactly what happens when I open a pull request.

49:08.040 --> 49:09.460
Step one is the borrow.

49:10.180 --> 49:15.420
The GitHub Action lane builds your new candidate image and pushes it to an Azure container registry.

49:16.160 --> 49:19.560
Then it runs a CLI command spy service pin.

49:20.080 --> 49:20.700
What does that do?

49:20.920 --> 49:27.940
It authenticates using the federated identity, reaches into the cluster, and writes an ephemeral pin to that image lock config map.

49:27.940 --> 49:33.960
It says, for the storage service, ignore the stable git manifest for a moment and run my new candidate image digest instead.

49:34.520 --> 49:40.300
And it notes the GitHub run ID directly on the pin, so everyone knows exactly which CI pipeline requested the change.

49:40.460 --> 49:45.440
As we said, Flux sees this change to the config map and instantly rolls the cluster pod to the new image.

49:45.580 --> 49:45.760
Right.

49:46.040 --> 49:47.600
Step two is the proof.

49:47.900 --> 49:50.780
But the GitHub lane can't just assume the pod started successfully, right?

49:51.080 --> 49:52.760
A bad image might crash loop.

49:52.760 --> 49:53.320
Exactly.

49:53.740 --> 49:57.000
So the lane pulls the cluster using spy service verify.

49:57.580 --> 50:05.880
It continually checks the live pod in the cluster until the pod actually reports back that its running image ID matches the newly built digest.

50:06.400 --> 50:10.800
Once the pod is verified as running the new code, it has to test the API.

50:11.180 --> 50:13.080
And testing an API requires authentication.

50:13.080 --> 50:13.560
Yes.

50:13.920 --> 50:18.340
So the lane mints three distinct UNTRA ID tokens using the environment fax.

50:18.680 --> 50:24.820
It mints one token with full resolve or admin access, one as a standard member, and one with no access whatsoever.

50:25.120 --> 50:26.500
Why three different tokens?

50:26.660 --> 50:29.700
Why not just use the admin token to prove the endpoints work?

50:29.940 --> 50:38.060
To prove that the endpoints not only work, but that the authorization rules are correctly enforced by the Envoy filter and the application across different permission levels.

50:38.060 --> 50:40.320
It runs the test suite three times.

50:40.520 --> 50:43.500
It proves that the no access token actually gets rejected.

50:43.860 --> 50:45.360
It validates the security boundary.

50:45.620 --> 50:47.280
And then step three, the restore.

50:47.460 --> 50:51.840
Crucially, after the tests run, the lane executes spy service reset, if run.

50:52.060 --> 50:57.000
And it runs this command always, even if the tests failed, even if the workflow crashed halfway through.

50:57.180 --> 50:58.400
The system cleans up its own loan.

50:58.800 --> 50:59.320
It does.

50:59.460 --> 51:02.020
It removes the ephemeral pin from the config map.

51:02.020 --> 51:14.140
And because the pin is gone, Flux automatically kicks in again, sees that the config map no longer overrides the git manifest, and rolls the cluster back to the canonical stable community image.

51:14.260 --> 51:17.020
It is exactly like a scientific peer review process.

51:17.140 --> 51:17.340
Yeah.

51:17.480 --> 51:19.500
You submit your paper, the new candidate image.

51:20.060 --> 51:25.280
The environment sets up the exact lab conditions by dynamically joining the descriptor and the fax.

51:25.280 --> 51:28.780
It runs the rigorous experiment with the three distinct tokens.

51:29.100 --> 51:37.020
And then, regardless of whether the experiment succeeded or the beaker exploded, it meticulously cleans the lab bench and restores the baseline for the next scientist.

51:37.440 --> 51:42.820
There is one critical detail in this step that ensures the whole system doesn't collapse over time due to drift.

51:43.260 --> 51:50.220
Before the GitHub Action Lane does any of this pinning and verifying, it checks the environment's stack version, say, v1.2.3.

51:50.220 --> 51:55.560
It then ensures that the Python CLI tooling running inside the GitHub Action is that exact same version.

51:55.820 --> 51:58.600
It forces the client version to match the server version.

51:58.860 --> 52:00.600
It matches the client to the server.

52:01.020 --> 52:10.060
This prevents tooling drift, ensuring the contract between the two repositories, the factory and the infrastructure, is truly versioned in practice, not just in theory.

52:10.320 --> 52:13.440
You never have a v2 script trying to talk to a v1 cluster.

52:13.840 --> 52:14.400
Amazing.

52:15.100 --> 52:16.480
Okay, we're in the homestretch.

52:16.480 --> 52:28.100
We have explored the entire life cycle, but no system is perfect, and one of the things I love most about this source material is how refreshingly honest the documentation is about its own flaws and failures.

52:28.540 --> 52:30.580
We have to look at the hard lessons they learned.

52:30.980 --> 52:33.920
First off, the documentation clearly states a golden rule.

52:34.340 --> 52:39.100
If the design document and the architectural decision records disagree, the ADRs govern.

52:39.320 --> 52:42.000
That is a profound statement about engineering reality.

52:42.000 --> 52:44.100
The design document describes intentions.

52:44.400 --> 52:47.160
It describes idealized capabilities from before the code was written.

52:47.380 --> 52:50.140
The ADRs are the actual state machine of the architecture.

52:50.360 --> 52:56.080
They record what was actually built, what compromises were forced by reality, and why they abandoned their initial designs.

52:56.360 --> 53:01.700
And then they have a whole list of things that are explicitly not true, despite what a new engineer might assume.

53:02.520 --> 53:04.940
For instance, fork upstream is not a mirror.

53:05.400 --> 53:07.360
We cover this as a computer-generated tree.

53:07.360 --> 53:10.460
Upstream bug fixes to Azure will not arrive automatically.

53:11.000 --> 53:12.340
But here's the one that caught my eye.

53:12.860 --> 53:16.680
Ready true does not mean OSDU is answering requests.

53:17.240 --> 53:17.500
Wait.

53:17.840 --> 53:23.020
So the documentation openly admits that some green status checks in their automation are basically lying.

53:23.660 --> 53:27.180
And they also admit that tearing down an environment leaves orphan resources behind.

53:27.580 --> 53:29.680
If you know it's broken, why not just fix the script?

53:29.680 --> 53:33.240
This speaks to the maturity of the engineering team and their documentation.

53:33.980 --> 53:37.780
Admitting a failure mode is far safer than pretending edge cases don't exist.

53:38.220 --> 53:39.600
Let's take the ready true status.

53:39.900 --> 53:43.860
That status just means that Flux finished reconciling the Kubernetes manifests.

53:44.120 --> 53:45.260
The infrastructure is deployed.

53:45.640 --> 53:45.920
Yes.

53:46.360 --> 53:47.920
But remember the schema loader.

53:48.040 --> 53:51.460
It has to load 1,300 schemas into Cosmos DB.

53:51.900 --> 53:55.760
That might take another hour to actually populate the database after Flux reports ready.

53:55.760 --> 54:00.340
If the documentation pretended that ready meant the API is fully functional,

54:00.860 --> 54:04.440
engineers would waste hours debugging phantom networking issues,

54:04.520 --> 54:06.860
trying to figure out why their queries are returning empty,

54:07.040 --> 54:10.140
when the reality is just that the database is still loading.

54:10.320 --> 54:12.560
Setting expectations prevents wild goose choices.

54:12.980 --> 54:14.000
And the teardown issue.

54:14.340 --> 54:15.780
Why do they leave resources behind?

54:16.320 --> 54:20.820
Azure ARM deployments and bicep teardowns aren't perfect atomic transactions.

54:21.320 --> 54:24.560
A teardown might fail halfway through due to a lock or a timeout,

54:24.560 --> 54:28.420
but specifically they document exactly what gets left behind deliberately,

54:28.740 --> 54:30.000
like certain managed identities.

54:30.560 --> 54:34.620
They keep those identities around to avoid breaking downstream onboarded repositories

54:34.620 --> 54:36.180
that might still be referencing them.

54:36.640 --> 54:37.960
By documenting this reality,

54:38.100 --> 54:42.180
they train service owners to troubleshoot the actual system as it behaves in the real world,

54:42.460 --> 54:45.040
not an idealized theoretical version of it.

54:45.240 --> 54:47.340
Brutal honesty is an engineering virtue.

54:47.480 --> 54:47.920
I love it.

54:48.000 --> 54:49.160
Okay, let's bring this all together.

54:49.500 --> 54:51.540
We have gone on a massive journey today.

54:51.540 --> 54:56.420
We started with a permanent generated fork that actively avoids Git merge conflicts

54:56.420 --> 55:00.940
by leveraging low-level plumbing to act like a filter rather than a snapshot.

55:01.500 --> 55:03.460
We explored a securely bootstrapped,

55:03.600 --> 55:07.380
pennish-heavy Azure cluster that relies entirely on workload identity

55:07.380 --> 55:09.460
and refuses to hold on to static secrets.

55:09.980 --> 55:12.360
And we unpacked a brilliant headless handshake

55:12.360 --> 55:17.260
that tests new code on live infrastructure without ever leaking cluster admin rights.

55:17.260 --> 55:21.080
It is a remarkable synthesis of Git mechanics, Kubernetes state management,

55:21.240 --> 55:22.260
and identity architecture.

55:22.620 --> 55:26.780
It represents hundreds of hours of trial and error distilled into a highly resilient system.

55:27.040 --> 55:27.780
It really is.

55:28.440 --> 55:32.400
And I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over as you begin your work as a service owner.

55:33.200 --> 55:36.960
Think about how this architecture completely redefines the concept of a fork.

55:37.840 --> 55:40.700
We are so used to thinking of code forks as snapshots,

55:41.120 --> 55:43.500
a frozen moment in time where we copy a project,

55:43.860 --> 55:46.100
change a few lines, and walk away to build our own thing.

55:46.100 --> 55:50.400
But the engineering system we looked at today turned a fork into a living relationship.

55:51.420 --> 55:53.140
It forces us to ask a tough question.

55:54.080 --> 55:58.080
In our own projects, are we just blindly copying dependencies and hoping for the best?

55:58.580 --> 56:03.100
Or are we engineering resilient systems that can continuously negotiate with an upstream world

56:03.100 --> 56:05.900
that might literally delete the ground we walk on tomorrow?

56:06.720 --> 56:10.780
When you get handed the keys to a complex system, you want it to be built like this one,

56:11.300 --> 56:13.940
honest about its flaws, relentless in its automation,

56:13.940 --> 56:16.380
and prepared for the upstream temple to collapse.

56:16.760 --> 56:17.940
Thanks for diving deep with us.

